  "notes": "USAGE:\nUsed either as a suru verb ({確信|かくしん}する = to be convinced) or with {持|も}つ ({確信|かくしん}を{持|も}つ = to have conviction). The phrase {確信犯|かくしんはん} originally means someone who commits a crime out of political or ideological conviction, but in modern colloquial use it often means someone who knowingly does something wrong.\n\nKANJI:\n{確|かく} means \"certain\" and {信|しん} means \"believe.\" The compound means \"certain belief.\"\n\nHOMOPHONE:\nNot to be confused with {核心|かくしん} (core, heart of a matter) or {革新|かくしん} (reform, innovation).\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {確信|かくしん}を{持|も}つ: to have conviction\n- {確信|かくしん}に{変|か}わる: to turn into conviction\n- {確信犯|かくしんはん}: deliberate offender",
